        "content": "# Requirements Specification: YAML Validator Command\n\n**Feature ID**: yaml-validator-command\n**Version**: 1.0.0\n**Status**: Draft\n**Created**: 2026-03-08\n\n## 1. Feature Overview\n\nThe project needs a command-line capability that allows a developer or user to validate YAML files for syntactic correctness directly from the CLI, using only the project's existing dependencies. This provides a lightweight, zero-dependency validation tool integrated into the existing utility scripting project.\n\n## 2. Business Context\n\n### 2.1 Problem Statement\n\nDevelopers working with YAML configuration files need a quick way to check whether a YAML file is syntactically valid before using it in downstream processes. Currently, the project offers no such capability, requiring developers to rely on external tools or online validators.\n\n### 2.2 Business Value\n\n- Reduces context-switching by keeping YAML validation within the project toolchain\n- Avoids introducing external dependencies, keeping the project footprint minimal\n- Provides immediate, actionable feedback on YAML syntax errors\n\n### 2.3 Success Metrics\n\n| Metric | Target |\n|--------|--------|\n| Valid YAML correctly identified | 100% of well-formed YAML files accepted |\n| Invalid YAML correctly rejected | Syntax errors detected with meaningful message |\n| Dependency count change | Zero new dependencies added |\n| Command execution time | Sub-second for typical config files (under 10KB) |\n\n## 3. Stakeholders & Users\n\n### 3.1 User Types\n\n| User Type | Description | Primary Need |\n|-----------|-------------|--------------|\n| Developer | A developer using this CLI tool in their workflow | Validate YAML files quickly from the terminal |\n| CI/CD Pipeline | Automated process consuming the command | Programmatic validation with exit codes |\n\n### 3.2 Stakeholder Interests\n\n| Stakeholder | Interest |\n|-------------|----------|\n| Project Maintainers | Keep dependency footprint at zero external packages |\n| End Users (Developers) | Reliable, fast YAML validation with clear error output |\n\n## 4. Scope Definition\n\n### 4.1 In Scope\n\n- A CLI command that accepts a file path argument and validates the YAML content\n- Clear success output when YAML is valid\n- Clear error output with descriptive messaging when YAML is invalid\n- Appropriate exit codes (0 for valid, non-zero for invalid/error)\n- Validation of YAML syntax (well-formedness)\n\n### 4.2 Out of Scope\n\n- YAML schema validation (validating against a specific schema or structure)\n- YAML linting (style checking, best practices enforcement)\n- YAML formatting or auto-fix capabilities\n- Support for multi-document YAML streams (may be deferred)\n- Network-based YAML fetching (URLs as input)\n- Adding any new npm dependencies to the project\n- GUI or interactive mode\n\n### 4.3 Assumptions\n\n- The Bun runtime provides sufficient built-in capability to parse YAML or the implementation can use a self-contained approach without new packages\n- YAML files to be validated are local files accessible from the filesystem\n- Input files are of reasonable size (configuration files, not multi-gigabyte data files)\n\n## 5. Functional Requirements\n\n### FR-001: Accept File Path Input\n- **Priority**: Must Have\n- **User Type**: Developer\n- **Requirement**: The command must accept a file path as a command-line argument specifying the YAML file to validate\n- **Rationale**: Developers need to point the tool at specific files in their project\n- **Acceptance Criteria**:\n  - GIVEN a valid file path argument, WHEN the command is invoked, THEN the file at that path is read and processed\n  - GIVEN no file path argument, WHEN the command is invoked, THEN a usage message is displayed and the process exits with a non-zero code\n\n### FR-002: Validate YAML Syntax\n- **Priority**: Must Have\n- **User Type**: Developer\n- **Requirement**: The command must parse the provided file content and determine whether it is syntactically valid YAML\n- **Rationale**: Core purpose of the feature -- detecting syntax errors before downstream consumption\n- **Acceptance Criteria**:\n  - GIVEN a file containing valid YAML, WHEN validated, THEN the command reports success\n  - GIVEN a file containing invalid YAML (e.g., bad indentation, malformed mappings, unclosed quotes), WHEN validated, THEN the command reports failure with an error description\n\n### FR-003: Report Validation Results\n- **Priority**: Must Have\n- **User Type**: Developer, CI/CD Pipeline\n- **Requirement**: The command must output a clear, human-readable message indicating whether the file is valid or what error was found\n- **Rationale**: Users need to understand the outcome without ambiguity; automation needs parseable results\n- **Acceptance Criteria**:\n  - GIVEN valid YAML, WHEN the command completes, THEN a success message is printed to stdout (e.g., \"Valid YAML: <filepath>\")\n  - GIVEN invalid YAML, WHEN the command completes, THEN an error message is printed to stderr including the nature of the error\n\n### FR-004: Return Appropriate Exit Codes\n- **Priority**: Must Have\n- **User Type**: CI/CD Pipeline\n- **Requirement**: The command must exit with code 0 on successful validation and a non-zero exit code on failure\n- **Rationale**: Enables integration into scripts and CI/CD pipelines that rely on exit codes\n- **Acceptance Criteria**:\n  - GIVEN valid YAML, WHEN the command exits, THEN the exit code is 0\n  - GIVEN invalid YAML, WHEN the command exits, THEN the exit code is 1\n  - GIVEN a missing or unreadable file, WHEN the command exits, THEN the exit code is 1\n\n### FR-005: Handle Missing or Unreadable Files\n- **Priority**: Must Have\n- **User Type**: Developer\n- **Requirement**: The command must gracefully handle cases where the specified file does not exist or cannot be read\n- **Rationale**: Prevents confusing crashes and provides actionable feedback\n- **Acceptance Criteria**:\n  - GIVEN a file path that does not exist, WHEN the command is invoked, THEN an error message like \"File not found: <path>\" is displayed and exit code is non-zero\n  - GIVEN a file path that is not readable, WHEN the command is invoked, THEN an appropriate permission error message is displayed\n\n### FR-006: Zero New Dependencies\n- **Priority**: Must Have\n- **User Type**: Project Maintainers\n- **Requirement**: The feature must be implemented without adding any new entries to package.json dependencies or devDependencies (beyond what already exists)\n- **Rationale**: Explicit constraint from the requirement to keep the project lightweight\n- **Acceptance Criteria**:\n  - GIVEN the implementation is complete, WHEN package.json is inspected, THEN no new dependencies have been added compared to the current state\n\n### FR-007: Invocable via CLI Script\n- **Priority**: Should Have\n- **User Type**: Developer\n- **Requirement**: The command should be easily invocable through a package.json script or directly via Bun\n- **Rationale**: Consistent with the project's existing pattern of `bun run` scripts\n- **Acceptance Criteria**:\n  - GIVEN the feature is implemented, WHEN a developer runs the designated command (e.g., `bun run validate <file>`), THEN the YAML validation executes\n\n## 6. Non-Functional Requirements\n\n### 6.1 Performance Expectations\n\n| Expectation | Target |\n|-------------|--------|\n| Validation of a typical config file (under 10KB) | Under 500ms total execution time |\n| Startup overhead | Minimal; consistent with existing Bun script startup |\n\n### 6.2 Security Requirements\n\n- The command must only read files; it must not write, modify, or delete any files\n- File access must be limited to the path explicitly provided by the user\n\n### 6.3 Usability Requirements\n\n- Error messages must be clear and actionable, indicating what went wrong and where\n- Usage instructions must be displayed when the command is invoked incorrectly\n- Output must be suitable for both human reading and basic script consumption\n\n### 6.4 Compliance Requirements\n\n- No additional compliance requirements identified for this feature\n\n## 7. User Stories\n\n### STORY-001: Validate a YAML Config File\n**As a** developer\n**I want** to run a command that checks my YAML file for syntax errors\n**So that** I can catch mistakes before using the file in my application or deployment\n\n**Acceptance Criteria**:\n- GIVEN I have a YAML file at `config.yml`, WHEN I run the validate command with `config.yml` as an argument, THEN I see either a success confirmation or a description of the syntax error\n\n### STORY-002: Integrate Validation into CI\n**As a** CI/CD pipeline operator\n**I want** the YAML validation command to return proper exit codes\n**So that** my pipeline can automatically fail when YAML configuration files are malformed\n\n**Acceptance Criteria**:\n- GIVEN a valid YAML file, WHEN the validation command runs in CI, THEN the step passes (exit code 0)\n- GIVEN an invalid YAML file, WHEN the validation command runs in CI, THEN the step fails (exit code non-zero)\n\n### STORY-003: Get Help When Misusing the Command\n**As a** developer unfamiliar with the tool\n**I want** to see usage instructions when I invoke the command incorrectly\n**So that** I can quickly understand how to use it\n\n**Acceptance Criteria**:\n- GIVEN no arguments are provided, WHEN the command is invoked, THEN a usage hint is printed to stderr\n\n## 8. Business Rules\n\n| Rule ID | Rule | Rationale |\n|---------|------|-----------|\n| BR-001 | No new npm dependencies may be introduced | Explicit project constraint; keeps footprint minimal |\n| BR-002 | The command must operate on local files only | Security and simplicity; network fetching is out of scope |\n| BR-003 | Empty files should be treated as valid YAML | An empty document is valid YAML per the specification |\n\n## 9. Dependencies & Constraints\n\n| Dependency/Constraint | Type | Impact |\n|-----------------------|------|--------|\n| Bun runtime | Runtime dependency | Command must run under Bun; already present |\n| No new npm packages | Hard constraint | Implementation must use only Bun built-ins and hand-written code |\n| Existing project structure | Soft constraint | Should follow existing patterns (kebab-case files, camelCase functions, co-located tests) |\n| TypeScript strict mode | Soft constraint | Consistent with project conventions |\n\n## 10. Clarifications Log\n\n| # | Question | Resolution | Source |\n|---|----------|------------|--------|\n| 1 | What does \"simple\" mean in scope? | Single command, validates syntax only, no schema/lint | Inferred from \"simple\" + project context |\n| 2 | Who is the user? | Developers running CLI and CI/CD pipelines | Inferred from CLI utility project domain |\n| 3 | What output format? | Human-readable text to stdout/stderr with exit codes | Inferred from CLI conventions |\n| 4 | What about empty files? | Treated as valid (empty document is valid YAML) | YAML specification default |\n\n---\n\n## AFK Mode: Auto-Selected Defaults\n\n| Decision Point | Choice | Rationale |\n|----------------|--------|-----------|\n| PRD selection | None (no PRDs available) | No PRD files exist in the project |\n| Charter association | None (no charter available) | No charter.md found |\n| Command style | CLI with file path argument | Consistent with existing `src/index.ts` pattern of reading `process.argv` |\n| Error output channel | stderr for errors, stdout for success | Standard CLI convention |\n\n## AFK Mode: Inferred Decisions\n\n| Ambiguity | Resolution | Source |\n|-----------|------------|--------|\n| \"simple\" -- vague scope qualifier | Single-purpose syntax validation only; no schema validation, linting, or formatting | Conservative default; \"simple\" interpreted as minimal feature set |\n| \"yaml validator command\" -- unclear form factor | CLI command invoked via Bun script, consistent with existing entry point pattern | KB: index.md shows CLI entry point pattern; patterns.md confirms Bun scripts |\n| \"without adding new deps\" -- scope of constraint | No new entries in package.json dependencies or devDependencies | KB: package.json has only @types/bun; patterns.md states \"No external dependencies beyond Bun built-ins\" |\n| Actor not specified | Developer (primary) and CI/CD pipeline (secondary) | Inferred from CLI tool domain and project description |\n| Success/failure criteria undefined | Valid YAML returns exit 0 with success message; invalid returns exit 1 with error description | Standard CLI validation tool conventions |\n| Multi-document YAML handling | Out of scope for initial version | Conservative default for \"simple\" requirement |\n"
      },
      "source": "assistant"
    },
