        "content": "# Design Decisions: YAML Validator Command\n\n**Feature ID**: yaml-validator-command\n**Created**: 2026-03-08\n\n## Decision Log\n\n| ID | Decision | Choice | Rationale | Alternatives Considered |\n|----|----------|--------|-----------|------------------------|\n| D1 | YAML parsing approach | Custom line-based syntax validator | Zero-dependency constraint forbids npm packages; Bun has no built-in YAML parser; line-based approach is sufficient for syntax checking of config files | Full recursive descent parser (too complex for feature scope), regex-only approach (insufficient for nested structures and state tracking) |\n| D2 | Module structure | Separate validator module (`yaml-validator.ts`) + CLI entry point (`validate-yaml.ts`) | Follows existing project pattern of separating pure logic (utils.ts) from entry points (index.ts); enables isolated unit testing of validation logic without CLI concerns | Single monolithic file (violates separation pattern), extending existing utils.ts (unrelated functionality would bloat the module) |\n| D3 | File reading API | `Bun.file()` API with `.exists()` and `.text()` | Native Bun file API; aligns with zero-dependency constraint; provides built-in existence checking; async-capable | Node.js `fs.readFileSync` (works under Bun but not idiomatic), `fs.promises.readFile` (also works but less aligned with Bun patterns) |\n| D4 | Entry point naming | `src/validate-yaml.ts` | kebab-case per project naming convention in patterns.md; verb-noun pattern describes the action; mirrors `index.ts` as a directly runnable script entry point | `src/cli.ts` (too generic for a project that may gain more CLI tools), `src/yaml-cli.ts` (less descriptive of action), `src/validator.ts` (ambiguous -- what does it validate?) |\n| D5 | Error reporting granularity | Line number, column number, and descriptive message per error | Requirements FR-003 specifies \"clear, human-readable message\" and \"nature of the error\"; NFR 6.3 requires \"actionable\" errors \"indicating what went wrong and where\"; line/column enables quick navigation to the problem | Message-only without location (less actionable, fails NFR 6.3), AST-level error recovery (over-engineered for a syntax checker) |\n| D6 | YAML specification coverage | Common configuration-file subset of YAML 1.2 | Requirements repeatedly use \"simple\" and scope is \"syntax validation\" for config files; full YAML 1.2 spec comprises complex features (anchors, tags, multi-document streams) impractical to implement without a library; common config patterns cover the practical use case | Full YAML 1.2 compliance (impractical without external parser library, violates BR-001), YAML 1.1 (same complexity issue), JSON-superset only (too restrictive, misses common YAML patterns like bare strings) |\n| D7 | Package.json script name | `validate` | Short, descriptive, consistent with existing script naming (single-word: `dev`, `test`, `lint`, `format`); invoked as `bun run validate <file>` | `validate-yaml` (verbose but more specific), `check-yaml` (valid alternative, less standard), `yaml` (too ambiguous) |\n| D8 | Test co-location | `src/yaml-validator.test.ts` alongside `src/yaml-validator.ts` | Matches existing project pattern where `utils.test.ts` sits next to `utils.ts`; patterns.md explicitly states \"Tests co-located with source as `*.test.ts`\" | Separate `test/` directory (violates project convention), `__tests__/` directory (also violates convention) |\n\n## AFK Mode: Auto-Selected Technology Decisions\n\n| Decision | Choice | Source | Rationale |\n|----------|--------|--------|-----------|\n| Runtime | Bun | KB: index.md | Project already uses Bun; no alternative considered |\n| Language | TypeScript with strict mode | KB: patterns.md | Project convention; tsconfig.json confirms `\"strict\": true` |\n| Testing framework | `bun:test` with describe/test/expect | KB: patterns.md | Existing project pattern; no other test runner present |\n| File naming convention | kebab-case | KB: patterns.md | Explicitly documented as project convention |\n| Function naming convention | camelCase | KB: patterns.md | Explicitly documented as project convention |\n| Type naming convention | PascalCase | KB: patterns.md | Explicitly documented as project convention |\n| Module system | ESM (`import`/`export`) | KB: patterns.md, package.json | `\"type\": \"module\"` in package.json; existing code uses ESM |\n| File I/O approach | `Bun.file()` API | Codebase analysis | Bun-native API; consistent with zero-dependency constraint |\n| Error handling pattern | Simple try/catch with descriptive messages | KB: patterns.md | Documented pattern: \"Simple try/catch with descriptive error messages\" |\n| YAML parsing strategy | Custom implementation (no deps) | KB: patterns.md, requirements FR-006 | \"No external dependencies beyond Bun built-ins\" is a documented pattern and explicit requirement |\n"
