        "file_path": "/private/tmp/rp1-evals/8e6cc059/workspace/.rp1/work/features/yaml-validator-command/design.md",
        "content": "# Technical Design: YAML Validator Command\n\n**Feature ID**: yaml-validator-command\n**Version**: 2.0.0\n**Created**: 2026-03-08\n**Updated**: 2026-03-08 (post-hypothesis validation)\n**Status**: Draft\n\n---\n\n## 1. Design Overview\n\nA CLI command that validates YAML files for syntactic correctness, implemented as a thin wrapper around `Bun.YAML.parse()` -- the Bun runtime's built-in YAML parser. The tool reads a file path from CLI arguments, attempts to parse the content via `Bun.YAML.parse()`, and reports success or failure with appropriate exit codes.\n\nThis design replaces the v1.0 custom line-based parser approach after hypothesis validation confirmed that Bun v1.3.3 provides a built-in YAML parser with >90% YAML 1.2 spec compliance (see HYP-001 REJECTED).\n\n### High-Level Architecture\n\n```mermaid\ngraph LR\n    CLI[\"CLI Entry Point<br/>validate-yaml.ts\"] --> ArgParser[\"Argument Parsing<br/>(process.argv)\"]\n    ArgParser --> FileReader[\"File Reader<br/>(Bun.file API)\"]\n    FileReader --> Validator[\"YAML Validator<br/>yaml-validator.ts\"]\n    Validator --> BunYAML[\"Bun.YAML.parse()<br/>(Built-in Parser)\"]\n    BunYAML --> Reporter[\"Result Reporter<br/>(stdout/stderr)\"]\n    Reporter --> ExitCode[\"Exit Code<br/>(process.exit)\"]\n```\n\nThe design follows the existing project pattern of a dedicated entry point file that imports pure utility functions from a separate module.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Architecture\n\n### Component Architecture\n\n```mermaid\ngraph TB\n    subgraph \"CLI Layer\"\n        VY[\"src/validate-yaml.ts<br/>Entry Point\"]\n    end\n\n    subgraph \"Logic Layer\"\n        YV[\"src/yaml-validator.ts<br/>Wrapper around Bun.YAML.parse\"]\n    end\n\n    subgraph \"Bun Runtime\"\n        BY[\"Bun.YAML.parse()<br/>Built-in YAML Parser\"]\n    end\n\n    subgraph \"Test Layer\"\n        YVT[\"src/yaml-validator.test.ts<br/>Unit Tests\"]\n    end\n\n    VY -->|imports| YV\n    YV -->|calls| BY\n    YVT -->|imports| YV\n```\n\n### Component Responsibilities\n\n| Component | File | Responsibility |\n|-----------|------|----------------|\n| CLI Entry Point | `src/validate-yaml.ts` | Argument parsing, file reading, output formatting, exit codes |\n| YAML Validator | `src/yaml-validator.ts` | Wraps `Bun.YAML.parse()` in a result-returning interface; converts thrown SyntaxError into a structured result |\n| Bun.YAML.parse | (runtime built-in) | Actual YAML parsing and syntax validation (YAML 1.2, Zig-based) |\n| Tests | `src/yaml-validator.test.ts` | Unit tests for the validator wrapper behavior |\n\n### Sequence Diagram -- Validation Flow\n\n```mermaid\nsequenceDiagram\n    participant User\n    participant CLI as validate-yaml.ts\n    participant FS as Bun.file API\n    participant Val as yaml-validator.ts\n    participant Parser as Bun.YAML.parse()\n\n    User->>CLI: bun run validate filepath\n    CLI->>CLI: Parse process.argv[2]\n\n    alt No argument provided\n        CLI->>User: stderr: Usage message\n        CLI->>CLI: process.exit(1)\n    end\n\n    CLI->>FS: Bun.file(filepath).exists()\n\n    alt File not found\n        CLI->>User: stderr: File not found: filepath\n        CLI->>CLI: process.exit(1)\n    end\n\n    FS-->>CLI: file content (string)\n    CLI->>Val: validateYaml(content)\n    Val->>Parser: Bun.YAML.parse(content)\n\n    alt Parse succeeds\n        Parser-->>Val: parsed value\n        Val-->>CLI: { valid: true }\n        CLI->>User: stdout: Valid YAML: filepath\n        CLI->>CLI: process.exit(0)\n    else Parse throws SyntaxError\n        Parser-->>Val: SyntaxError\n        Val-->>CLI: { valid: false, error: message }\n        CLI->>User: stderr: Error description\n        CLI->>CLI: process.exit(1)\n    end\n```\n\n---\n\n## 3. Detailed Design\n\n### 3.1 Data Model\n\n```typescript\n/** Result of YAML validation */\ninterface ValidationResult {\n  valid: boolean;\n  error?: string;\n}\n```\n\nThe v1.0 design used `ValidationError[]` with `line`, `column`, and `message` fields. This is simplified because `Bun.YAML.parse()` does not expose line/column positions within the YAML content on thrown errors. The `error` field contains the descriptive message from the `SyntaxError` thrown by the parser (e.g., \"YAML Parse error: Unexpected EOF\").\n\n### 3.2 YAML Validator Module (`src/yaml-validator.ts`)\n\nExports:\n\n| Function | Signature | Purpose |\n|----------|-----------|---------|\n| `validateYaml` | `(content: string) => ValidationResult` | Main entry point -- validates YAML string via Bun.YAML.parse() |\n\nImplementation logic:\n\n1. **Empty document handling**: Empty string or whitespace-only content is valid YAML (BR-003). Check for this case first and return `{ valid: true }` without calling the parser, ensuring consistent behavior regardless of parser edge cases.\n2. **Parse attempt**: Call `Bun.YAML.parse(content)` inside a try/catch.\n3. **Success path**: If `Bun.YAML.parse()` returns without throwing, return `{ valid: true }`.\n4. **Error path**: If `Bun.YAML.parse()` throws a `SyntaxError`, catch it and return `{ valid: false, error: error.message }`.\n5. **Unexpected error path**: If a non-SyntaxError is thrown, re-format it as a generic validation error string and return `{ valid: false, error: \"Unexpected error: ...\" }`.\n\n**YAML spec coverage**: `Bun.YAML.parse()` provides YAML 1.2 compliance with >90% of the official YAML test suite passing. This includes support for anchors, aliases, tags, multi-line scalars, flow collections, and document markers -- far exceeding what the v1.0 custom parser could cover.\n\n**Known characteristics of Bun.YAML.parse()**:\n- Tab indentation is treated leniently (parsed rather than rejected).\n- Duplicate keys silently use the last value.\n- Error messages are descriptive but lack line/column position within the YAML content.\n\n### 3.3 CLI Entry Point (`src/validate-yaml.ts`)\n\nExecution flow:\n\n1. Read `process.argv[2]` for file path.\n2. If no argument: print usage to stderr, exit 1.\n3. Create `Bun.file(filepath)` reference.\n4. Check existence via `.exists()`. If not found: print \"File not found: {path}\" to stderr, exit 1.\n5. Read content via `.text()` in a try/catch. If read fails: print permission/read error to stderr, exit 1.\n6. Call `validateYaml(content)`.\n7. If `result.valid`: print \"Valid YAML: {filepath}\" to stdout, exit 0.\n8. If not valid: print error message to stderr, exit 1.\n\n**Usage message format**:\n```\nUsage: bun run validate <filepath>\n\nValidates a YAML file for syntactic correctness.\n```\n\n**Error output format**:\n```\nYAML validation error in <filepath>:\n  <error message from parser>\n```\n\nNote: The v1.0 design included \"Line N, Column M:\" prefixes. These are removed because `Bun.YAML.parse()` does not provide line/column positions for YAML content errors. The parser's error message itself is descriptive (e.g., \"YAML Parse error: Unexpected token\").\n\n---\n\n## 4. Technology Stack\n\n| Category | Choice | Rationale |\n|----------|--------|-----------|\n| Runtime | Bun v1.3.3 | Existing project runtime |\n| Language | TypeScript (strict mode) | Existing project language |\n| YAML parsing | `Bun.YAML.parse()` | Built-in Bun YAML parser; zero-dependency; YAML 1.2 compliant; confirmed via HYP-001 validation |\n| File I/O | `Bun.file()` API | Bun-native, async-capable file reading |\n| CLI args | `process.argv` | Existing pattern in `src/index.ts` |\n| Testing | `bun:test` | Existing project test framework |\n| Dependencies | None new | Hard constraint FR-006 / BR-001 |\n\n---\n\n## 5. Implementation Plan\n\n| # | Component | Description | Files Modified/Created |\n|---|-----------|-------------|----------------------|\n| T1 | YAML Validator Module | Wrapper around `Bun.YAML.parse()` -- exports `validateYaml()` function and `ValidationResult` type. Handles empty content check, try/catch around parser, and error message extraction from SyntaxError. | Create: `src/yaml-validator.ts` |\n| T2 | CLI Entry Point | Script that reads CLI args, reads file via Bun.file, calls `validateYaml()`, formats output to stdout/stderr, sets exit code. | Create: `src/validate-yaml.ts` |\n| T3 | Unit Tests | Tests for `validateYaml()` covering: wrapper behavior on valid input, wrapper behavior on invalid input, empty content handling (BR-003), and error message propagation. | Create: `src/yaml-validator.test.ts` |\n| T4 | Package.json Script | Add `\"validate\"` script entry pointing to `bun run src/validate-yaml.ts`. | Modify: `package.json` |\n\n---\n\n## 6. Implementation DAG\n\n**Parallel Groups** (tasks with no inter-dependencies):\n\n1. [T1] - Foundation module with no dependencies on other new components\n2. [T2, T3] - Both depend only on T1; CLI entry and tests are independent of each other\n3. [T4] - Package.json update references the CLI entry point from T2\n\n**Dependencies**:\n\n- T2 -> T1 (Interface: CLI imports `validateYaml` from yaml-validator module)\n- T3 -> T1 (Interface: Tests import `validateYaml` from yaml-validator module)\n- T4 -> T2 (Build: Script entry references `src/validate-yaml.ts`)\n\n**Critical Path**: T1 -> T2 -> T4\n\n---\n\n## 7. Testing Strategy\n\n### Test Value Assessment\n\n| Test Category | Value | Rationale |\n|---------------|-------|-----------|\n| Wrapper returns valid for well-formed YAML | HIGH | Core business logic -- confirms our wrapper correctly interprets parser success |\n| Wrapper returns invalid with error for malformed YAML | HIGH | Core business logic -- confirms our wrapper correctly catches and surfaces parser errors |\n| Empty content handling | HIGH | Business rule BR-003 explicit requirement; our code's explicit empty-check logic |\n| Error message propagation | MEDIUM | App-specific behavior -- confirms SyntaxError message reaches the caller |\n| Unexpected error handling | LOW | Defensive code path; unlikely in practice |\n| CLI argument handling | LOW | Thin wrapper; tested via integration if needed |\n| File reading errors | LOW | Bun.file API behavior; light smoke test sufficient |\n\n### Test Plan (`src/yaml-validator.test.ts`)\n\nTests target the `validateYaml` function (pure function, easy to unit test).\n\n**Valid YAML cases** (wrapper returns `{ valid: true }`):\n- Simple key-value mapping (`key: value`)\n- Nested mappings\n- Sequences (list items with `- `)\n- Mixed mappings and sequences\n- Empty content (BR-003)\n- Whitespace-only content (BR-003)\n\n**Invalid YAML cases** (wrapper returns `{ valid: false, error: <string> }`):\n- Unclosed flow collection (e.g., `[a, b` without closing bracket)\n- Malformed mapping structure\n- Content that triggers Bun.YAML.parse() SyntaxError\n\n**Wrapper behavior tests**:\n- Error field is a non-empty string when validation fails\n- Error field is undefined when validation succeeds\n- Valid field is boolean in all cases\n\n### Tests NOT included (per test value assessment):\n\nThese would test Bun's parser behavior rather than our application code:\n- Exhaustive YAML 1.2 feature coverage (anchors, aliases, tags, etc.)\n- Bun.YAML.parse() performance characteristics\n- Bun.YAML.parse() specific error message wording (implementation detail of Bun runtime)\n- Bun.file() read behavior verification\n- process.argv parsing framework behavior\n- process.exit() call verification\n\n---\n\n## 8. Deployment Design\n\nNo special deployment required. The feature integrates into the existing project structure.\n\n| Aspect | Detail |\n|--------|--------|\n| Invocation | `bun run validate <filepath>` |\n| Script entry | `package.json` -> `\"validate\": \"bun run src/validate-yaml.ts\"` |\n| Runtime requirement | Bun >= 1.2.21 (for Bun.YAML support; project uses 1.3.3) |\n| Build step | None (Bun runs TypeScript directly) |\n| Environment variables | None |\n\n---\n\n## 9. Documentation Impact\n\n| Type | Target | Section | KB Source | Rationale |\n|------|--------|---------|-----------|-----------|\n| edit | `.rp1/context/index.md` | Project Structure | index.md:Project Structure | New files added to `src/` |\n| edit | `.rp1/context/modules.md` | Modules | modules.md:Modules | Two new modules: yaml-validator, validate-yaml |\n| edit | `.rp1/context/architecture.md` | Scripts | architecture.md:Scripts | New `validate` script added |\n\n---\n\n## 10. Design Decisions Log\n\n| ID | Decision | Choice | Rationale | Alternatives Considered |\n|----|----------|--------|-----------|------------------------|\n| D1 | YAML parsing approach | `Bun.YAML.parse()` built-in parser | HYP-001 confirmed Bun v1.3.3 provides a built-in YAML 1.2 parser with >90% spec compliance; satisfies zero-dependency constraint; dramatically simpler and more correct than a custom parser | Custom line-based parser (v1.0 design -- far more complex, limited spec coverage, more bugs), hybrid approach with custom error location scanner (added complexity for marginal benefit) |\n| D2 | Module structure | Separate validator module + CLI entry point | Follows existing pattern (utils.ts + index.ts separation); enables unit testing of wrapper logic independently from CLI concerns | Single file (violates separation of concerns), adding to existing utils.ts (unrelated functionality) |\n| D3 | File reading API | `Bun.file()` API | Native Bun API, aligns with zero-dependency constraint, async-capable | Node.js `fs.readFileSync` (works but not Bun-idiomatic) |\n| D4 | Entry point file name | `src/validate-yaml.ts` | kebab-case per project pattern; descriptive of purpose; mirrors `index.ts` as a runnable script | `src/cli.ts` (too generic), `src/yaml-cli.ts` (less descriptive) |\n| D5 | Error reporting granularity | Parser error message without line/column | `Bun.YAML.parse()` SyntaxError does not include YAML-content line/column positions (confirmed via HYP-001 experiments); the error message text is descriptive enough for actionable feedback (e.g., \"YAML Parse error: Unexpected token\"); adding a custom line scanner for position-only info would add complexity disproportionate to benefit | Line/column via custom scanner (added complexity, fragile heuristic), generic \"invalid YAML\" message (less actionable) |\n| D6 | YAML spec coverage | Full YAML 1.2 via Bun built-in | `Bun.YAML.parse()` provides >90% YAML 1.2 test suite compliance including anchors, aliases, tags, multi-line scalars, and directives -- far exceeding what any reasonable custom implementation could achieve | Custom subset parser (v1.0 design -- limited to common config patterns only) |\n| D7 | Data model simplification | Single optional error string instead of error array with positions | Parser throws one SyntaxError on first issue; no line/column available; array of positioned errors is not achievable with `Bun.YAML.parse()` | Keep v1.0 ValidationError[] with line/column (not possible with built-in parser), parse error message for position hints (fragile regex, not reliable) |\n| D8 | Empty content handling | Explicit check before calling parser | BR-003 requires empty files to be valid; explicit check guarantees this regardless of parser behavior on empty strings | Rely on parser behavior (less predictable across versions) |\n\n### Changes from v1.0 Design\n\n| Area | v1.0 | v2.0 | Reason |\n|------|------|------|--------|\n| Parser | Custom line-based | `Bun.YAML.parse()` | HYP-001 rejected: Bun has built-in YAML parser |\n| YAML coverage | Config-file subset | YAML 1.2 (>90%) | Built-in parser provides full spec |\n| Error detail | Line, column, message | Message only | Built-in parser lacks position info |\n| Data model | `ValidationError[]` | `error?: string` | Single error from parser, no positions |\n| Complexity | ~200+ lines of parsing logic | ~20 lines wrapper | Delegated to Bun runtime |\n| D1 rationale | \"Bun has no built-in YAML parser\" | \"Bun provides built-in YAML parser\" | Hypothesis validation |\n| D6 rationale | \"Full spec impractical without library\" | \"Full spec via built-in\" | Built-in is a \"library\" bundled with runtime |\n"
