        "content": "# Design Decisions: YAML Validator Command\n\n**Feature ID**: yaml-validator-command\n**Created**: 2026-03-08\n**Updated**: 2026-03-08 (v2.0 -- post-hypothesis validation)\n\n## Decision Log\n\n| ID | Decision | Choice | Rationale | Alternatives Considered |\n|----|----------|--------|-----------|------------------------|\n| D1 | YAML parsing approach | `Bun.YAML.parse()` built-in parser | HYP-001 validation confirmed Bun v1.3.3 provides a built-in YAML 1.2 parser (Zig-based, >90% spec compliance). This satisfies the zero-dependency constraint (FR-006/BR-001) while providing far superior correctness and spec coverage compared to a custom implementation. The validator becomes a thin wrapper (~20 lines) instead of ~200+ lines of custom parsing logic. | Custom line-based syntax validator (v1.0 choice -- rejected after HYP-001 proved Bun has a built-in parser; far more complex, limited spec coverage, higher defect risk), full recursive descent parser (massive over-engineering), hybrid approach using Bun.YAML.parse() for validation + custom scanner for error positions (added complexity for marginal benefit given parser errors are already descriptive) |\n| D2 | Module structure | Separate validator module (`yaml-validator.ts`) + CLI entry point (`validate-yaml.ts`) | Follows existing project pattern of separating pure logic (utils.ts) from entry points (index.ts); enables isolated unit testing of validation wrapper without CLI concerns | Single monolithic file (violates separation pattern), extending existing utils.ts (unrelated functionality would bloat the module) |\n| D3 | File reading API | `Bun.file()` API with `.exists()` and `.text()` | Native Bun file API; aligns with zero-dependency constraint; provides built-in existence checking; async-capable | Node.js `fs.readFileSync` (works under Bun but not idiomatic), `fs.promises.readFile` (also works but less aligned with Bun patterns) |\n| D4 | Entry point naming | `src/validate-yaml.ts` | kebab-case per project naming convention in patterns.md; verb-noun pattern describes the action; mirrors `index.ts` as a directly runnable script entry point | `src/cli.ts` (too generic for a project that may gain more CLI tools), `src/yaml-cli.ts` (less descriptive of action), `src/validator.ts` (ambiguous -- what does it validate?) |\n| D5 | Error reporting granularity | Parser error message text without line/column positions | `Bun.YAML.parse()` throws `SyntaxError` with descriptive messages (e.g., \"YAML Parse error: Unexpected EOF\", \"YAML Parse error: Unexpected token\") but does NOT include line/column positions within the YAML content (confirmed via HYP-001 code experiments -- `originalLine` and `originalColumn` are `undefined`). The message text is sufficiently actionable for the target use case (config file validation). Adding a custom line scanner solely for position reporting would introduce significant complexity for marginal user benefit. | Line/column via custom post-error scanner (fragile heuristic matching, high complexity, unreliable for all error types), generic \"invalid YAML\" without parser message (less actionable, fails NFR 6.3) |\n| D6 | YAML specification coverage | Full YAML 1.2 via `Bun.YAML.parse()` | Built-in parser provides >90% YAML 1.2 test suite compliance including anchors, aliases, tags, multi-line scalars, flow collections, directives, and document markers. This far exceeds the v1.0 \"common config-file subset\" approach and eliminates entire categories of correctness bugs. | Custom config-file subset parser (v1.0 -- limited coverage, many edge cases missed), JSON-superset only (too restrictive for real YAML files) |\n| D7 | Data model simplification | `ValidationResult` with `valid: boolean` and `error?: string` | `Bun.YAML.parse()` throws a single `SyntaxError` on the first parse failure; there is no mechanism to collect multiple errors. Line/column positions are not available from the parser. The v1.0 `ValidationError[]` with `line`, `column`, `message` fields is not achievable with the built-in parser. A single optional error string is the natural model. | Keep v1.0 `ValidationError[]` array (not populated by built-in parser), parse error.message via regex for position hints (fragile and unreliable) |\n| D8 | Empty content handling | Explicit check before calling parser | Business rule BR-003 requires empty files to be treated as valid YAML. An explicit `content.trim() === \"\"` check before calling `Bun.YAML.parse()` guarantees this behavior regardless of how the parser handles empty strings across Bun versions. | Rely on `Bun.YAML.parse(\"\")` behavior (may vary across versions; defensive explicit check is more robust) |\n| D9 | Package.json script name | `validate` | Short, descriptive, consistent with existing script naming (single-word: `dev`, `test`, `lint`, `format`); invoked as `bun run validate <file>` | `validate-yaml` (verbose but more specific), `check-yaml` (valid alternative, less standard), `yaml` (too ambiguous) |\n| D10 | Test co-location | `src/yaml-validator.test.ts` alongside `src/yaml-validator.ts` | Matches existing project pattern where `utils.test.ts` sits next to `utils.ts`; patterns.md explicitly states \"Tests co-located with source as `*.test.ts`\" | Separate `test/` directory (violates project convention), `__tests__/` directory (also violates convention) |\n\n## Superseded Decisions (from v1.0)\n\n| Original ID | Original Choice | New Choice | Reason for Change |\n|-------------|----------------|------------|-------------------|\n| D1 (v1.0) | Custom line-based syntax validator | `Bun.YAML.parse()` built-in | HYP-001 rejected: rationale \"Bun has no built-in YAML parser\" was incorrect; Bun v1.2.21+ provides `Bun.YAML.parse()` |\n| D5 (v1.0) | Line and column numbers with description | Message text only | `Bun.YAML.parse()` SyntaxError lacks YAML-content line/column positions |\n| D6 (v1.0) | Common config-file subset of YAML 1.2 | Full YAML 1.2 (>90%) | Built-in parser provides comprehensive spec coverage |\n\n## AFK Mode: Auto-Selected Technology Decisions\n\n| Decision | Choice | Source | Rationale |\n|----------|--------|--------|-----------|\n| Runtime | Bun v1.3.3 | KB: index.md | Project already uses Bun; no alternative considered |\n| Language | TypeScript with strict mode | KB: patterns.md | Project convention; tsconfig.json confirms `\"strict\": true` |\n| YAML parser | `Bun.YAML.parse()` | HYP-001 validation evidence | Built-in Bun API confirmed available in installed version; zero dependencies; superior to custom parser |\n| Testing framework | `bun:test` with describe/test/expect | KB: patterns.md | Existing project pattern; no other test runner present |\n| File naming convention | kebab-case | KB: patterns.md | Explicitly documented as project convention |\n| Function naming convention | camelCase | KB: patterns.md | Explicitly documented as project convention |\n| Type naming convention | PascalCase | KB: patterns.md | Explicitly documented as project convention |\n| Module system | ESM (`import`/`export`) | KB: patterns.md, package.json | `\"type\": \"module\"` in package.json; existing code uses ESM |\n| File I/O approach | `Bun.file()` API | Codebase analysis | Bun-native API; consistent with zero-dependency constraint |\n| Error handling pattern | Simple try/catch with descriptive messages | KB: patterns.md | Documented pattern: \"Simple try/catch with descriptive error messages\" |\n| Error detail level | Parser message text (no line/column) | HYP-001 evidence | Bun.YAML.parse() SyntaxError lacks positional info; accepted trade-off for dramatically simpler implementation |\n| Data model | Simplified `{ valid, error? }` | HYP-001 evidence + design analysis | Parser provides single error on failure; positions unavailable; array model from v1.0 not achievable |\n"
