        "content": "# Requirements Specification: YAML Validator Command\n\n**Feature ID**: yaml-validator-command\n**Version**: 1.0.0\n**Status**: Draft\n**Created**: 2026-03-08\n\n## 1. Feature Overview\n\nThe project needs a command-line capability that allows developers to validate YAML files for syntactic correctness directly from the terminal, using only the existing technology stack with no additional dependencies. This provides a lightweight, built-in validation step that can be incorporated into development and CI workflows.\n\n## 2. Business Context\n\n### 2.1 Problem Statement\n\nDevelopers working with YAML configuration files currently have no built-in way to verify that their YAML is syntactically valid within this project's toolset. Malformed YAML can cause silent failures or confusing errors downstream. A dedicated validation command gives immediate, clear feedback on YAML correctness.\n\n### 2.2 Business Value\n\n- Reduces time spent debugging configuration errors caused by invalid YAML syntax.\n- Provides a self-contained validation tool that does not increase the project's dependency footprint.\n- Fits naturally into the existing project structure as a reusable utility command.\n\n### 2.3 Success Metrics\n\n| Metric | Target |\n|--------|--------|\n| Valid YAML files are correctly accepted | 100% of well-formed YAML input is reported as valid |\n| Invalid YAML files produce a clear error | 100% of malformed YAML input results in a descriptive error message |\n| No new runtime or dev dependencies introduced | Zero new entries in package.json dependencies or devDependencies (beyond existing) |\n| Command is runnable via a single npm/bun script | Accessible via `bun run` script |\n\n## 3. Stakeholders & Users\n\n### 3.1 User Types\n\n| User Type | Description | Relevance |\n|-----------|-------------|-----------|\n| Developer | A developer using this project who authors or edits YAML files | Primary user; runs the validator to check file correctness |\n| CI Pipeline | An automated build/test pipeline | Secondary consumer; may invoke the validator as a pre-check step |\n\n### 3.2 Stakeholder Interests\n\n| Stakeholder | Interest |\n|-------------|----------|\n| Project Maintainers | Keep the project lean (no new deps); consistent patterns |\n| Developers | Fast, clear feedback on YAML validity |\n\n## 4. Scope Definition\n\n### 4.1 In Scope\n\n- Validating YAML files for syntactic correctness (well-formedness).\n- Accepting a file path as input.\n- Reporting success or failure with a human-readable message.\n- Providing a clear, non-zero exit code on validation failure.\n- Adding a runnable script entry (e.g., `bun run validate-yaml`).\n- Unit tests for the core validation logic.\n\n### 4.2 Out of Scope\n\n- YAML schema validation (e.g., validating against a JSON Schema or custom schema).\n- Batch validation of multiple files in a single invocation.\n- Auto-fixing or reformatting invalid YAML.\n- Support for YAML features beyond standard syntax (e.g., custom tags).\n- Interactive or TUI-based validation interface.\n- Reading from stdin (may be considered in a future iteration).\n\n### 4.3 Assumptions\n\n- Bun's runtime provides sufficient built-in capabilities to parse YAML without external libraries (or a simple hand-written parser can handle common YAML syntax).\n- The YAML files to be validated are of reasonable size (not multi-gigabyte).\n- The project will continue using Bun as its runtime.\n\n## 5. Functional Requirements\n\n### REQ-001: Accept File Path Input\n- **Priority**: Must Have\n- **User Type**: Developer\n- **Requirement**: The validator command must accept a file path as a command-line argument specifying the YAML file to validate.\n- **Rationale**: Developers need to point the tool at a specific file for validation, consistent with how the existing entry point accepts CLI arguments.\n- **Acceptance Criteria**:\n  - AC-1: Running the command with a valid file path reads and processes that file.\n  - AC-2: Running the command without a file path argument produces a usage message explaining expected input.\n\n### REQ-002: Validate YAML Syntax\n- **Priority**: Must Have\n- **User Type**: Developer\n- **Requirement**: The command must determine whether the contents of the specified file constitute syntactically valid YAML.\n- **Rationale**: This is the core purpose of the feature -- giving developers confidence that their YAML is well-formed.\n- **Acceptance Criteria**:\n  - AC-1: A file containing valid YAML produces a success message (e.g., \"Valid YAML\").\n  - AC-2: A file containing invalid YAML (e.g., bad indentation, unclosed quotes, invalid characters) produces an error message describing the problem.\n\n### REQ-003: Report Validation Results Clearly\n- **Priority**: Must Have\n- **User Type**: Developer\n- **Requirement**: The command must output a clear, human-readable message indicating whether validation succeeded or failed, and in the failure case, include information about what went wrong.\n- **Rationale**: Developers need actionable feedback to locate and fix errors in their YAML files.\n- **Acceptance Criteria**:\n  - AC-1: On success, a message confirming validity is printed to stdout.\n  - AC-2: On failure, an error message is printed to stderr that includes at minimum a description of the syntax error.\n\n### REQ-004: Exit Code Reflects Outcome\n- **Priority**: Must Have\n- **User Type**: CI Pipeline\n- **Requirement**: The command must exit with code 0 on successful validation and a non-zero exit code on validation failure or error.\n- **Rationale**: CI pipelines and scripts rely on exit codes to determine pass/fail status.\n- **Acceptance Criteria**:\n  - AC-1: Exit code is 0 when the YAML file is valid.\n  - AC-2: Exit code is 1 (or another non-zero value) when the YAML file is invalid.\n  - AC-3: Exit code is non-zero when the specified file does not exist or cannot be read.\n\n### REQ-005: Handle Missing or Unreadable Files\n- **Priority**: Must Have\n- **User Type**: Developer\n- **Requirement**: The command must gracefully handle cases where the specified file does not exist or cannot be read, producing a clear error message.\n- **Rationale**: Developers may mistype paths; the tool should fail gracefully rather than crashing with a stack trace.\n- **Acceptance Criteria**:\n  - AC-1: If the file does not exist, the command outputs an error message stating the file was not found.\n  - AC-2: The command exits with a non-zero exit code when the file is missing or unreadable.\n\n### REQ-006: No New Dependencies\n- **Priority**: Must Have\n- **User Type**: Project Maintainers\n- **Requirement**: The implementation must not introduce any new entries in package.json `dependencies` or `devDependencies` (beyond what already exists).\n- **Rationale**: Explicitly stated constraint in the requirements. Keeps the project lean and avoids supply-chain risk.\n- **Acceptance Criteria**:\n  - AC-1: The `dependencies` and `devDependencies` fields in package.json remain unchanged (no new packages).\n\n### REQ-007: Runnable via Script Command\n- **Priority**: Should Have\n- **User Type**: Developer\n- **Requirement**: The validator should be runnable via a named script in package.json (e.g., `bun run validate-yaml <filepath>`).\n- **Rationale**: Consistent with how the project exposes its existing `dev` and `test` commands; makes discovery easy.\n- **Acceptance Criteria**:\n  - AC-1: A script entry exists in package.json that invokes the validator.\n  - AC-2: Running the script with a file path argument validates that file.\n\n### REQ-008: Unit Tests for Validation Logic\n- **Priority**: Should Have\n- **User Type**: Project Maintainers\n- **Requirement**: The core validation logic should have unit tests covering valid YAML, invalid YAML, and edge cases.\n- **Rationale**: Consistent with the project's existing testing pattern; ensures regression safety.\n- **Acceptance Criteria**:\n  - AC-1: Tests exist that verify valid YAML input is accepted.\n  - AC-2: Tests exist that verify invalid YAML input is rejected with appropriate error information.\n  - AC-3: Tests pass when run via `bun test`.\n\n## 6. Non-Functional Requirements\n\n### 6.1 Performance Expectations\n\n| Expectation | Target |\n|-------------|--------|\n| Validation of a typical YAML file (under 1 MB) | Completes in under 1 second |\n| Startup overhead | Minimal; no heavy initialization |\n\n### 6.2 Security Requirements\n\n- The command must only read the file specified by the user; it must not write to or modify any files.\n- No network access is required or permitted during validation.\n\n### 6.3 Usability Requirements\n\n- Error messages must be understandable to a developer without requiring knowledge of parser internals.\n- The command must provide a usage hint when invoked without arguments.\n\n### 6.4 Compliance Requirements\n\n- No additional compliance requirements identified for this feature.\n\n## 7. User Stories\n\n### STORY-001: Validate a YAML Configuration File\n- **As a** developer\n- **I want to** run a command that checks whether my YAML file is syntactically correct\n- **So that** I can catch formatting errors before they cause problems downstream\n\n**Acceptance (GIVEN/WHEN/THEN)**:\n- GIVEN a YAML file exists on disk\n- WHEN the developer runs the validate-yaml command with that file path\n- THEN the command outputs whether the file is valid or invalid, with error details if invalid\n\n### STORY-002: Catch Invalid YAML Early\n- **As a** developer\n- **I want to** receive a clear error message when my YAML has syntax errors\n- **So that** I can quickly locate and fix the problem\n\n**Acceptance (GIVEN/WHEN/THEN)**:\n- GIVEN a YAML file with a syntax error (e.g., bad indentation)\n- WHEN the developer runs the validate-yaml command\n- THEN the output includes a description of the syntax error and the command exits with a non-zero code\n\n### STORY-003: Integrate Validation into CI\n- **As a** CI pipeline\n- **I want to** invoke the YAML validator and rely on its exit code\n- **So that** builds fail automatically when YAML configuration is malformed\n\n**Acceptance (GIVEN/WHEN/THEN)**:\n- GIVEN a CI step runs the validate-yaml command against a YAML file\n- WHEN the file is invalid\n- THEN the command exits with a non-zero code, causing the CI step to fail\n\n### STORY-004: Handle Missing File Gracefully\n- **As a** developer\n- **I want to** see a helpful error when I provide a wrong file path\n- **So that** I know the file was not found rather than seeing a cryptic crash\n\n**Acceptance (GIVEN/WHEN/THEN)**:\n- GIVEN no file exists at the specified path\n- WHEN the developer runs the validate-yaml command with that path\n- THEN the command outputs a \"file not found\" message and exits with a non-zero code\n\n## 8. Business Rules\n\n| Rule ID | Rule | Rationale |\n|---------|------|-----------|\n| BR-001 | The validator checks syntax only, not semantic correctness or schema conformance | Keeps scope minimal and achievable without external dependencies |\n| BR-002 | No new packages may be added to the project | Explicit constraint from requirements; maintains project simplicity |\n| BR-003 | The command follows existing project patterns for CLI arguments and code style | Maintains consistency across the codebase |\n\n## 9. Dependencies & Constraints\n\n| Dependency / Constraint | Type | Impact |\n|-------------------------|------|--------|\n| Bun runtime | Runtime dependency (existing) | Must use Bun built-in capabilities for YAML parsing |\n| No new npm packages | Hard constraint | Validation logic must be implemented using only existing dependencies and built-ins |\n| TypeScript strict mode | Convention | All new code must adhere to project TypeScript conventions |\n| Co-located tests | Convention | Tests must follow `*.test.ts` pattern next to source files |\n\n## 10. Clarifications Log\n\n| Item | Question | Resolution | Source |\n|------|----------|------------|--------|\n| CL-001 | What does \"simple\" mean for the validator? | Minimal, single-purpose command; syntax validation only | Inferred from \"simple\" + no-new-deps constraint |\n| CL-002 | Who are the users? | Developers and CI pipelines | Inferred from project type (utility scripting) |\n| CL-003 | Is this a subcommand or standalone script? | Standalone script entry, consistent with existing `src/index.ts` pattern | Inferred from project structure |\n| CL-004 | What level of YAML validation? | Syntax/well-formedness only; no schema validation | Conservative inference from \"simple\" + no-deps constraint |\n| CL-005 | What is the input mechanism? | File path via CLI argument | Inferred from existing `process.argv` pattern in index.ts |\n| CL-006 | Should it support stdin? | Out of scope for MVP | Conservative scoping decision |\n\n---\n\n## AFK Mode: Auto-Selected Defaults\n\n| Decision Point | Choice | Rationale |\n|----------------|--------|-----------|\n| PRD selection | No PRD available | No PRD files found in `.rp1/work/prds/`; feature proceeds without parent PRD |\n| Validation scope | Syntax-only validation | \"Simple\" requirement + no-new-deps constraint makes schema validation impractical and out of scope |\n| Input method | File path via CLI argument | Matches existing project pattern (process.argv in index.ts) |\n| Output format | Human-readable text to stdout/stderr | Simplest approach consistent with existing project conventions |\n| Script name | `validate-yaml` | Follows kebab-case naming convention from patterns.md |\n\n## AFK Mode: Inferred Decisions\n\n| Ambiguity | Resolution | Source |\n|-----------|------------|--------|\n| \"simple\" (vague term) | Single-purpose CLI command with minimal features; syntax check only | Conservative default; consistent with project's minimalist nature (KB: index.md) |\n| Actors not specified | Primary: Developer; Secondary: CI pipeline | Inferred from project domain \"utility scripting\" (KB: concept_map.md) |\n| \"command\" type unclear | New script entry point, similar to existing `src/index.ts` pattern | KB: modules.md, project structure |\n| Validation depth undefined | Syntax/well-formedness only; no schema validation | Conservative default given no-deps constraint |\n| Input source undefined | File path argument via process.argv | KB: existing pattern in src/index.ts |\n| Error reporting detail undefined | Human-readable error description printed to stderr | KB: patterns.md (simple try/catch with descriptive error messages) |\n"
