        "file_path": "/private/tmp/rp1-evals/9ac4518c/workspace/.rp1/work/features/yaml-validator-command/design.md",
        "content": "# Technical Design: YAML Validator Command\n\n**Feature ID**: yaml-validator-command\n**Version**: 1.0.0\n**Created**: 2026-03-08\n**Status**: Draft\n\n---\n\n## 1. Design Overview\n\nA lightweight CLI command that validates YAML files for syntactic correctness. The command accepts a file path argument, reads the file, runs it through a hand-written YAML syntax validator, and reports the result with appropriate exit codes. No new dependencies are introduced -- all logic is implemented using only Bun built-ins and TypeScript.\n\n### High-Level Architecture\n\n```mermaid\ngraph LR\n    CLI[\"CLI Entry Point<br/>validate-yaml.ts\"] -->|reads| FS[\"File System<br/>Bun.file()\"]\n    CLI -->|calls| VAL[\"YAML Validator<br/>yaml-validator.ts\"]\n    VAL -->|returns| RES[\"ValidationResult<br/>{valid, errors}\"]\n    CLI -->|outputs| OUT[\"stdout / stderr\"]\n    CLI -->|sets| EXIT[\"process.exit(code)\"]\n    TEST[\"yaml-validator.test.ts\"] -->|tests| VAL\n```\n\n## 2. Architecture\n\nThe feature follows the existing single-layer script architecture. It introduces two new source files that mirror the existing `index.ts` / `utils.ts` separation pattern: a CLI entry point and a pure utility module.\n\n### Component Architecture\n\n```mermaid\ngraph TB\n    subgraph \"New Files\"\n        A[\"src/validate-yaml.ts<br/>(CLI Entry Point)\"]\n        B[\"src/yaml-validator.ts<br/>(Pure Validation Logic)\"]\n        C[\"src/yaml-validator.test.ts<br/>(Unit Tests)\"]\n    end\n    subgraph \"Existing Files\"\n        D[\"package.json<br/>(Script Entry)\"]\n    end\n\n    A -->|imports validateYaml| B\n    C -->|imports validateYaml| B\n    D -->|\"bun run src/validate-yaml.ts\"| A\n```\n\n### Component Descriptions\n\n| Component | Purpose | Pattern |\n|-----------|---------|---------|\n| `src/yaml-validator.ts` | Pure validation logic. Exports `validateYaml()` function that accepts a string and returns a structured result. | Mirrors `src/utils.ts` -- pure functions, no side effects |\n| `src/validate-yaml.ts` | CLI entry point. Handles argument parsing, file reading, output formatting, exit codes. | Mirrors `src/index.ts` -- script entry, uses `process.argv` |\n| `src/yaml-validator.test.ts` | Unit tests for the validation function. | Mirrors `src/utils.test.ts` -- co-located, `describe`/`test`/`expect` |\n| `package.json` (modified) | Adds `validate-yaml` script entry. | Follows existing script naming pattern |\n\n## 3. Detailed Design\n\n### 3.1 Data Model\n\n**ValidationResult** -- Return type of the core validation function:\n\n```typescript\ninterface ValidationError {\n  /** 1-based line number where the error was detected */\n  line: number;\n  /** Human-readable description of the syntax error */\n  message: string;\n}\n\ninterface ValidationResult {\n  /** Whether the YAML content is syntactically valid */\n  valid: boolean;\n  /** List of syntax errors found (empty if valid) */\n  errors: ValidationError[];\n}\n```\n\n### 3.2 Core Validation Logic (`src/yaml-validator.ts`)\n\n**Exported function**:\n\n```typescript\nexport function validateYaml(content: string): ValidationResult\n```\n\n**Validation checks performed** (in order):\n\n| Check | Description | Example Invalid Input |\n|-------|-------------|----------------------|\n| Tab indentation | YAML spec forbids tabs for indentation | `\\tkey: value` |\n| Unclosed quoted strings | Detect unmatched single or double quotes in scalar values | `key: \"unclosed value` |\n| Mismatched flow indicators | Unbalanced `[]` or `{}` in flow-style collections | `items: [a, b, c` |\n| Invalid mapping syntax | Lines that appear to be key-value pairs but have structural issues | `key value` (missing colon separator) |\n| Inconsistent indentation | Child items with indentation that does not align to a consistent block level | Mixed 2-space and 3-space nesting |\n| Document marker validation | Ensure `---` and `...` document markers are used correctly | `-- -` (malformed marker) |\n\n**Design notes**:\n- The validator processes the input line-by-line for simplicity and clarity.\n- Comment lines (starting with `#` after optional whitespace) and blank lines are skipped.\n- The validator does NOT attempt full YAML spec compliance. It targets common, practical syntax errors that developers encounter.\n- Empty input and whitespace-only input are treated as valid (empty YAML document).\n\n### 3.3 CLI Entry Point (`src/validate-yaml.ts`)\n\n**Flow**:\n\n```mermaid\ngraph TB\n    START[\"Start\"] --> ARGS[\"Read process.argv[2]\"]\n    ARGS -->|missing| USAGE[\"Print usage to stderr<br/>Exit code 1\"]\n    ARGS -->|present| READ[\"Read file via Bun.file(path).text()\"]\n    READ -->|error| FILEERR[\"Print file error to stderr<br/>Exit code 1\"]\n    READ -->|success| VALIDATE[\"Call validateYaml(content)\"]\n    VALIDATE -->|valid| SUCCESS[\"Print success to stdout<br/>Exit code 0\"]\n    VALIDATE -->|invalid| FAIL[\"Print errors to stderr<br/>Exit code 1\"]\n```\n\n**Output format**:\n\n| Scenario | Stream | Message |\n|----------|--------|---------|\n| Valid YAML | stdout | `Valid YAML: <filepath>` |\n| Invalid YAML | stderr | `Invalid YAML: <filepath>\\n  Line <N>: <error message>` (one line per error) |\n| File not found | stderr | `Error: File not found: <filepath>` |\n| File unreadable | stderr | `Error: Cannot read file: <filepath>` |\n| No argument | stderr | `Usage: bun run validate-yaml <filepath>` |\n\n**Exit codes**:\n\n| Code | Meaning |\n|------|---------|\n| 0 | YAML is valid |\n| 1 | YAML is invalid, file error, or usage error |\n\n### 3.4 Package.json Update\n\nAdd script entry:\n\n```json\n\"validate-yaml\": \"bun run src/validate-yaml.ts\"\n```\n\n## 4. Technology Stack\n\n| Category | Choice | Rationale |\n|----------|--------|-----------|\n| Language | TypeScript (strict mode) | Existing project language |\n| Runtime | Bun | Existing project runtime |\n| File I/O | `Bun.file().text()` | Bun-native async file reading |\n| Testing | `bun:test` | Existing test framework |\n| YAML Parsing | Hand-written syntax validator | No-new-deps constraint (REQ-006); YAML parsing libraries would require adding dependencies |\n\n## 5. Implementation Plan\n\n| # | Component | Description | Files |\n|---|-----------|-------------|-------|\n| T1 | Core Validation Module | Implement `validateYaml()` function with all syntax checks. Define `ValidationResult` and `ValidationError` types. | `src/yaml-validator.ts` |\n| T2 | CLI Entry Point | Implement argument parsing, file reading, result output, and exit code logic. | `src/validate-yaml.ts` |\n| T3 | Unit Tests | Write tests covering valid YAML, each type of syntax error, edge cases (empty file, whitespace-only), and error result structure. | `src/yaml-validator.test.ts` |\n| T4 | Package.json Script | Add `validate-yaml` script entry to package.json. | `package.json` |\n\n## 6. Implementation DAG\n\n**Parallel Groups** (tasks with no inter-dependencies):\n\n1. [T1] - Core validation module has no dependencies on other new components\n2. [T2, T3] - Both depend on T1 interfaces but are independent of each other\n3. [T4] - Script entry references T2's file path\n\n**Dependencies**:\n\n- T2 -> T1 (Interface: T2 imports `validateYaml` from T1)\n- T3 -> T1 (Interface: T3 imports `validateYaml` from T1 for testing)\n- T4 -> T2 (Build: script entry references T2's file path)\n\n**Critical Path**: T1 -> T2 -> T4\n\n## 7. Testing Strategy\n\n### Test Value Assessment\n\n| Test Category | Value | Rationale |\n|---------------|-------|-----------|\n| Valid YAML acceptance | HIGH | Core business logic -- verifies the tool works for its primary use case |\n| Invalid YAML detection (each error type) | HIGH | Core business logic -- verifies the tool catches syntax errors |\n| Error result structure | MEDIUM | Application-specific data contract between validator and CLI |\n| Edge cases (empty, whitespace-only) | MEDIUM | Application-specific boundary conditions |\n\n### Test Plan (`src/yaml-validator.test.ts`)\n\n| Test Group | Test Case | Input | Expected |\n|------------|-----------|-------|----------|\n| Valid YAML | Simple key-value | `key: value` | `{ valid: true, errors: [] }` |\n| Valid YAML | Nested mapping | Multi-level indented YAML | `{ valid: true, errors: [] }` |\n| Valid YAML | List items | `- item1\\n- item2` | `{ valid: true, errors: [] }` |\n| Valid YAML | Comments and blank lines | `# comment\\n\\nkey: value` | `{ valid: true, errors: [] }` |\n| Valid YAML | Empty input | `\"\"` | `{ valid: true, errors: [] }` |\n| Valid YAML | Flow-style collections | `items: [a, b, c]` | `{ valid: true, errors: [] }` |\n| Invalid YAML | Tab indentation | `\\tkey: value` | `{ valid: false, errors: [{ line: 1, ... }] }` |\n| Invalid YAML | Unclosed double quote | `key: \"unclosed` | `{ valid: false, errors: [{ line: 1, ... }] }` |\n| Invalid YAML | Unclosed single quote | `key: 'unclosed` | `{ valid: false, errors: [{ line: 1, ... }] }` |\n| Invalid YAML | Mismatched brackets | `items: [a, b` | `{ valid: false, errors: [...] }` |\n| Invalid YAML | Mismatched braces | `obj: {a: 1` | `{ valid: false, errors: [...] }` |\n| Edge Cases | Whitespace-only | `\"   \\n  \\n\"` | `{ valid: true, errors: [] }` |\n| Edge Cases | Document markers | `---\\nkey: value\\n...` | `{ valid: true, errors: [] }` |\n\n**Tests NOT included** (low value):\n- Testing Bun.file() behavior directly (framework behavior)\n- Testing process.argv parsing (runtime behavior)\n- Testing console.log/console.error output (library behavior)\n\n## 8. Deployment Design\n\nNo special deployment steps required. The feature consists of source files added to the existing project structure.\n\n**Rollout**:\n1. Merge new files into `src/` directory.\n2. Update `package.json` with the new script entry.\n3. Verify with `bun test` that all tests pass.\n4. Command becomes available via `bun run validate-yaml <filepath>`.\n\n**Rollback**: Revert the commit. No database migrations, 