        "new_string": "## Validation Findings\n\n### HYP-001 Findings\n**Validated**: 2026-03-08T15:24:00Z\n**Method**: CODE_EXPERIMENT\n**Result**: CONFIRMED\n\n**Evidence**:\n\nA prototype line-by-line YAML syntax validator was implemented in TypeScript (matching the project language) and tested against a corpus of 69 test cases spanning three test suites:\n\n1. **Baseline suite** (32 cases): Simple key-value, nested mappings, lists, flow sequences/mappings, nested flow collections, block scalar literal `|`, block scalar folded `>`, strip/keep chomping indicators `|-`/`|+`, anchors/aliases, comments, document markers, empty input, whitespace-only, quoted strings with special characters, list of mappings, deep nesting with mixed types, various scalar types, inline comments. Also 9 invalid cases covering tab indentation, unclosed quotes, mismatched brackets/braces. **Result: 32/32 passed (100%).**\n\n2. **Adversarial suite** (23 cases): Multi-line flow sequences/mappings, multi-line plain scalars, colons in unquoted URLs, brackets in unquoted values, block scalars with blank lines, 6-level deep nesting, list of lists, varying indentation amounts, special characters in keys, escape sequences, empty values, compact nested mappings, block scalar termination detection, folded scalar with strip, YAML tags, quoted keys, merge keys with multiple aliases, YAML directives. Also 3 invalid cases. **Result: 22/23 passed (95.7%). 1 false positive: multi-line double-quoted string (A18).**\n\n3. **Edge case suite** (14 cases): Multi-line single-quoted string, explicit newline escapes, quoted brackets in flow context, multiple colons (time format), complex keys with `?` indicator, block scalars with varying indentation, multi-line flow with quotes, aliases as list items, real-world Kubernetes deployment YAML, GitHub Actions workflow YAML, Docker Compose YAML, ESLint config YAML. Also 2 invalid cases. **Result: 13/14 passed (92.9%). 1 false positive: multi-line single-quoted string (E01).**\n\n**Grand total: 67/69 passed (97.1% accuracy, 2.9% failure rate).**\n\nThe 2 failures are both false positives (valid YAML incorrectly rejected) caused by the same root issue: **multi-line quoted strings** where a single-quoted or double-quoted string spans multiple lines. A strictly line-by-line parser sees the opening quote on one line and the closing quote on another line, and incorrectly reports unclosed quotes on each line.\n\nKey observations:\n- Block scalars (`|`, `>`, `|-`, `>-`, `|+`, `>2`) are handled correctly by tracking block scalar state across lines.\n- Multi-line flow collections (`[...\\n...]`, `{...\\n...}`) are handled correctly by tracking bracket/brace depth across lines.\n- All 4 real-world YAML patterns (Kubernetes, GitHub Actions, Docker Compose, ESLint) passed without issue.\n- The multi-line quoted string limitation is addressable by adding quote-state tracking across lines (similar to how bracket depth is already tracked), but adds complexity to the \"line-by-line\" approach.\n\n**Sources**:\n- Prototype: `/tmp/hypothesis-yaml-validator-command/yaml-validator-prototype.ts` (DISPOSABLE)\n- Baseline tests: `/tmp/hypothesis-yaml-validator-command/test-corpus.ts` (DISPOSABLE)\n- Adversarial tests: `/tmp/hypothesis-yaml-validator-command/test-adversarial.ts` (DISPOSABLE)\n- Edge case tests: `/tmp/hypothesis-yaml-validator-command/test-edge-cases.ts` (DISPOSABLE)\n\n**Implications for Design**:\nThe line-by-line approach is feasible for the targeted validation scope described in the design document. With a 97.1% accuracy rate across 69 diverse test cases (well below the 10% rejection threshold), the approach handles all common YAML patterns correctly including: nested mappings, flow collections, block scalars with all chomping indicators, anchors/aliases, document markers, and real-world configuration files. The known limitation (multi-line quoted strings) is a rare pattern in typical configuration YAML and can be mitigated by either (a) adding cross-line quote tracking, or (b) documenting it as an unsupported edge case. The design should note this limitation explicitly. The validator does NOT need to be a full spec-compliant parser to be practically useful for catching the most common YAML syntax errors.\n\n## Summary\n| Hypothesis | Risk | Result | Implication |\n|------------|------|--------|-------------|\n| HYP-001: Hand-written line-by-line YAML validator feasibility | HIGH | CONFIRMED | Line-by-line approach achieves 97.1% accuracy across 69 test cases. Known limitation: multi-line quoted strings (2 failures). Feasible for the targeted validation scope with this documented caveat. |\n"
